WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:04.390
There’s a lot to digest in Zack Snyder’s
Justice League, from the appearance of Martian

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:07.299
Manhunter to Lois Lane's pregnancy test revelation.

00:00:07.299 --> 00:00:12.120
However, there is one thing that almost instantly
became very clear from this version of the

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:18.090
movie, as compared to the original one finished
by Joss Whedon and released in 2017: Wonder

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.820
Woman gets her due.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:24.619
SyFy Wire noted that Diana became a "lame
rom-com protagonist," nagging, bickering,

00:00:24.619 --> 00:00:29.550
and flirting with Batman despite the way he
shoehorns Steve Trevor into a conversation

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.810
to get an emotional reaction out of her.

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:37.170
She became victim to gratuitous up-skirt shots
and her action scenes, oddly, were truncated.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.940
SyFy Wire said one of her action scenes, also,
seemed

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.560
"Clearly like a Whedon reshoot created for
fan service, as it has zero to do with the

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:45.780
wider plot."

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.340
Thanks to the Snyder Cut, though, that view
of Wonder Woman is gone.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:54.070
She now claims a front and center position
in the Justice League's battle against Steppenwolf,

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:57.260
showing that she was leading — with her
trademark compassion and care — the whole

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:58.260
time.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.030
So, let's really get into that scene when
Wonder Woman battles Steppenwolf and cuts

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:03.070
off his head.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:07.789
The scene takes place in Part 7, the final
confrontation between the heroes and Darkseid's

00:01:07.789 --> 00:01:08.789
minion.

00:01:08.789 --> 00:01:12.939
While every one of the heroes participates
in the standoff, Cyborg and the Flash's specialized

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:17.109
abilities mean they focus on making sure the
Unity never happens.

00:01:17.109 --> 00:01:21.759
The plan is for Flash to whip up a charge
that will allow Cyborg to disrupt the synchronization

00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:23.079
of the Mother Boxes.

00:01:23.079 --> 00:01:27.020
The others — Batman, Aquaman, and Wonder
Woman — are left to deal with a Steppenwolf

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:32.119
who seems especially to enjoy taunting Diana,
telling her she abandoned her sisters and

00:01:32.119 --> 00:01:33.789
bringing up her mother's death.

00:01:33.789 --> 00:01:36.840
Superman soon enters the fray, helping both
groups.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.659
When the plan fails, the Flash turns back
time to give everyone a second chance.

00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:44.909
In the end, Superman takes off Steppenwolf's
horn, and Aquaman spears him.

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:49.439
But it's Diana who gets in the killing blow
as Superman pushes the alien menace back toward

00:01:49.439 --> 00:01:52.340
the portal that will bring him back to Darkseid.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:56.850
Diana jumps in with her sword, slicing his
head off just before he enters the Boom Tube.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:01.039
This was the intended ending to Justice League
all along — and it's one of the reasons

00:02:01.039 --> 00:02:04.009
that fans are loving this restored version
of the character.

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:05.280
One user posted on Twitter,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.110
"This is easily the best movie that has ever
been made about Wonder Woman.

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:14.350
Her personality, culture, family, abilities,
strength, wisdom, etc.

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:18.280
She carries the action in the film, and they
give her a TON of screen time to showcase

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.720
her power in a way I've never seen anyone
do before."

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:26.650
The scene allows Diana to exhibit her fierceness
and boldness as a warrior, removing any doubt

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:30.610
about her abilities and bringing back a vision
of the character that should never have been

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.640
allowed to lapse, as it did in the original
Justice League.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:38.010
There's no doubt that Steppenwolf is a powerful
foe, requiring the combined efforts of heroes

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:41.890
like Superman and Wonder Woman, despite their
own formidable strengths.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.860
In DC Comics canon, Steppenwolf is a New God.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:49.340
Diana is a demigod: her father was Zeus, and
her mother was Hippolyta of the Amazons.

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:51.530
In this way, they are equals.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:55.480
This means that, to director Zack Snyder,
Wonder Woman killing Steppenwolf is basically

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:57.510
the only ending that makes any sense.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:02.540
By definition, gods can't be killed, at least
not by mere mortals — even ones with mutant

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:06.230
powers, like Aquaman, or ones who get theirs
from the yellow sun, like Superman.

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:11.840
In 2019, Snyder made a post on Vero that revealed
his concept of that climactic scene in three

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.180
unfinished images.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:14.720
In the caption, he noted,

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:19.620
"Not sure how they killed Steppenwolf in the
theatrical version of JL this was never finished

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.030
all the way but I use Gods to kill Gods."

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:26.590
It turns out, Snyder’s idea of Wonder Woman
seems so reasonable that it's the one many

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:28.960
others are embracing, too.

